{"acceptance_length": 5.416666666666667, "acceptance_lengths": [3, 2, 3, 1, 1, 6, 6, 2, 1, 5, 13, 16, 6, 1, 3, 2, 5, 3, 6, 6, 2, 11, 4, 7, 2, 1, 2, 2, 3, 6, 13, 5, 14, 3, 7, 5, 6, 1, 15, 15, 7, 3, 10, 4, 4, 6, 4, 7], "accepted_draft_lengths": [2, 1, 2, 0, 0, 5, 5, 1, 0, 4, 12, 15, 5, 0, 2, 1, 4, 2, 5, 5, 1, 10, 3, 6, 1, 0, 1, 1, 2, 5, 12, 4, 13, 2, 6, 4, 5, 0, 14, 14, 6, 2, 9, 3, 3, 5, 3, 6], "allocated_memory_after_bytes": 18525507072, "allocated_memory_before_bytes": 18525504000, "benchmark": "mtbench", "completion": "We are given three statements and asked to evaluate the **third statement** based on the **first two**.\n\n---\n\n### **Statements:**\n\n1. **Oranges cost more than apples.**  \n2. **Oranges cost less than bananas.**  \n3. **Bananas cost more than apples and bananas cost more than oranges.**\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Analyze the first two statements.**\n\n- **Statement 1:** Oranges > Apples  \n- **Statement 2:** Oranges < Bananas\n\nFrom these, we can deduce the following inequality chain:\n\n> **Bananas > Oranges > Apples**\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Evaluate the third statement.**\n\n**Statement 3:** *Bananas cost more than apples and bananas cost more than oranges.*\n\nLet\u2019s break it down:\n\n- **Bananas > Apples** \u2192 **True** (from the chain: Bananas > Oranges > Apples)\n- **Bananas > Oranges** \u2192 **True** (from the chain: Bananas > Oranges)\n\nSo, **both parts of the third statement are true**.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 **Conclusion:**\n\nIf the first two statements are true, then the third statement is **True**.\n\n", "decode_seconds": 1.695597777608782, "draft_calls": 48, "event": "benchmark_row", "input_tokens": 84, "mapper_checkpoint_sha256": "90b1a96af780856f0ed3e9377753b4e10b27972c5a5aea201c1a0d5322dfbbf5", "method": "relay_math_seed1729", "output_hash": "d01d6586a127ba555c68e754104ccc66907a72095dc5f88cccc5921e56e9ba0a", "output_tokens": 256, "peak_allocated_memory_bytes": 18636196352, "peak_reserved_memory_bytes": 18956156928, "problem